 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum-ii/",
 "pattern": "Backtracking: each number once, skip equal neighbours",
 "statement": "Given `candidates` (which **may contain duplicates**) and a `target`, return all unique combinations that add up to `target`. Each candidate may be used **at most once**.",
 "example": {
  "input": "candidates = [2,5,1,2], target = 5",
  "output": "[[1,2,2],[5]]",
  "why": "1 + 2 + 2 = 5 and 5 alone. [1, 2, 2] uses both 2s once each."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "candidates = [10,1,2,7,6,1,5], target = 8",
   "output": "[[1,1,6],[1,2,5],[1,7],[2,6]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "candidates = [2,5,2,1,2], target = 5",
   "output": "[[1,2,2],[5]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "candidates = [3], target = 1",
   "output": "[]"
  },
  {
   "input": "candidates = [1,1,1], target = 2",
   "output": "[[1,1]]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Like Combination Sum, track how much is **still needed**. But each number can be used only once, so after picking index `i` the next pick starts at `i + 1`.",
  "Duplicates in the input would produce the same combination several times. **Sort**, and at each slot skip a value equal to the one just tried there.",
  "Sorting gives a second win: as soon as a candidate is larger than what is needed, every later one is too — **stop** the loop."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def combinationSum2(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:\n        def backtrack(start, target, combination):\n            if target == 0:\n                result.append(combination[:])\n                return\n\n            for i in range(start, len(candidates)):\n                if i > start and candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]:\n                    continue  # same value already tried for this slot\n                if candidates[i] > target:\n                    break  # sorted: everything after is too large as well\n\n                combination.append(candidates[i])\n                backtrack(i + 1, target - candidates[i], combination)\n                combination.pop()\n\n        candidates.sort()\n        result = []\n        backtrack(0, target, [])\n        return result",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
  "timeWhy": "Each candidate is either in or out, and saving a combination costs O(n). Sorting and pruning cut this down a lot in practice.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "Recursion depth and the combination are at most n (not counting the output)."
 },
